\chapter{Introduction}

Modern general-purpose operating systems face a fundamental tension
between high scalability on many-core hardware and low-latency, formally
verifiable security guarantees. Traditional monolithic designs suffer
from lock contention, large Trusted Computing Bases (TCBs), and
inflexible kernel-user boundaries.

The \textbf{Telix} operating system synthesizes foundational microkernel
research, safe systems programming paradigms, and modern database memory
management techniques to establish a scalable, general-purpose system
architecture.

\section{Motivations}
\label{sec:motivations}

Telix exists at the intersection of three convictions, none of which
is unique to this project but whose combination has not been attempted
in a single system.

\subsection{The Page-Clustering\,/\,Superpaging Synergy}

The original motivating observation is that \pageclustering\ and
\superpaging\ solve each other's weaknesses.  Superpaging reduces TLB
pressure but is unreliable: hardware superpages fail under external
fragmentation, and the larger the superpage the rarer the contiguity.
\pageclustering\ eliminates external fragmentation at the allocation
unit $Z$, making smaller superpages ($Z$ and upward) reliable.
Conversely, \pageclustering\ alone only changes the physical-accounting
granularity; without superpaging to convert larger mappings into TLB
entries, the benefit never reaches the hardware.  Together they create
a regime where the allocation unit $Z$ becomes an attractor in the
translation-size distribution (\S\ref{sec:mem_clustering}), the modal
mapping size rises from the hardware base page to something much
larger, and TLB reach expands with it.

This interaction has been visible in fragmentary form since
Dickins' 2003 64\,GiB x86 boot, but it has never been explored
systematically at the design level: how does an entire memory
subsystem change when the \emph{reliable} allocation size is not the
hardware page but a multi-page extent?  Telix is the attempt to
answer that question from the ground up.

\subsection{Decisions the Literature Got Right (and the Market Got Wrong)}

Several design decisions that the systems-research community converged
on in the 1990s were abandoned not because they were incorrect but
because the enabling infrastructure did not yet exist, or because the
market's gravitational pull toward Linux compatibility made them
uneconomical to maintain.  Telix revisits them with the benefit of
two decades of hardware and language evolution:

\begin{description}
  \item[\textbf{M:N threading.}] Scheduler activations (Anderson et
    al., 1991) correctly identified the goal — lightweight user-level
    concurrency with kernel cooperation — but were implemented before
    io\_uring-style completion rings, cache-coherent shared memory,
    and language-level async abstractions existed.  The abandonment
    of M:N in Linux, FreeBSD, and Solaris was \emph{historically}
    correct; Telix argues the tools have caught up
    (\S\ref{sec:anderson_failure_modes}).

  \item[\textbf{The microkernel.}] Mach, L4, and their successors
    won the architectural argument — minimal kernel, services in
    userspace, capability-based IPC — and lost the market to
    Linux's monolithic momentum.  Telix's framekernel is a
    microkernel with a thin Rust-enforced in-kernel layer for
    latency-critical components; the microkernel structure
    remains the fallback if the framekernel boundary proves
    unsound (\S\ref{ch:control-plane}).

  \item[\textbf{Inverted page tables and dense size spectra.}]
    Architectures with software-managed TLBs (MIPS, SPARC, Itanium,
    POWER) can use inverted page tables sized to the number of
    \emph{mappings} rather than pages.  Dense superpage-size
    spectra ($W = 1$, dozens of distinct translation sizes) are
    natural in an inverted table and catastrophic in a radix
    tree.  Both ideas were abandoned when hardware converged on
    radix-tree walkers; Telix resurrects them as a research
    prototype (\S\ref{sec:custom_mmu}), in part to demonstrate
    that page clustering's benefits are not limited to the
    sparse spectra of x86 and ARM.

  \item[\textbf{Extent-based (not per-page) memory accounting.}]
    Main-memory databases abandoned per-row metadata decades ago;
    operating systems still maintain a \texttt{struct page} (or
    equivalent) for every 4\,KiB frame.  The morsel/LLFree
    allocator (\S\ref{sec:mem_coremapless}) eliminates the
    global descriptor array at the cost of a structural change
    to the allocator — a change that is impossible to retrofit
    into a mature kernel but straightforward in a new one.

  \item[\textbf{Safe-language whole-system construction.}]
    Singularity and Midori demonstrated that an entire operating
    system — kernel, drivers, filesystems, network stack — can
    be written in a safe, garbage-collected language and sealed
    into software-isolated processes without hardware MMU
    boundaries.  Both were research prototypes that never reached
    production; both proved the concept.  Telix extends this
    lineage with a key difference: rather than committing the
    whole system to one safe language, Telix's framekernel
    selectively pulls latency-critical components into a Rust
    address space while the rest — including filesystem servers,
    personality emulators, and device drivers written in
    languages as varied as Haskell, Mercury, and Koka — run as
    conventional microkernel processes.  The
    Frankenstein\,/\,Organ Bank polyglot compilation stack
    (Chapter~\ref{ch:polyglot}) is the toolchain that makes this
    heterogeneity mechanically real.
\end{description}

In each case, Telix's claim is not that the idea is new.  The claim is
that the idea was right, and that the combination of modern hardware,
Rust's type system, and a greenfield implementation makes it
implementable without the compromises that sank its predecessors.

\subsection{Formal Verification of Operating-System Algorithms}

The seL4 project proved it is possible to verify a microkernel's
correctness end-to-end.  CertiKOS extended this to a concurrent kernel
with fine-grained locking.  But neither project verified the
\emph{memory-management policy}: page replacement, superpage promotion
and demotion, the shootdown protocol, the interaction between the
allocator and the TLB coherence model.  These are the algorithms that
dictate whether a kernel performs well, not just whether it is
correct.

Telix's verification target (Chapter~\ref{ch:verification}) is the full
set of algorithms that interact with the MMU, the IOMMU, and the
interrupt controller — the page-table manipulator, the TLB shootdown
protocol, the IPI delivery mechanism, the IOMMU invalidation path, and
the LLFree allocation and free paths.  By modelling the hardware in
Sail and proving its invariants in Rocq/Iris independently of the
kernel (the \textsc{Tessera} project), and by writing the kernel's
specification directly in Iris heap\_lang and proving it against those
hardware invariants, the verification task decomposes into manageable
pieces: hardware proofs (\textasciitilde{}12,000 lines, already done),
a machine-interface Iris layer, and then kernel-function proofs one
function at a time.

This is not a claim that Telix is verified today.  It is a claim that
the verification \emph{strategy} — hardware model independent of
kernel, bridged by Iris resources, kernel proved against those
resources — is structurally different from the seL4 monolithic-proof
approach, and is suited to verifying the memory-management layer that
seL4 and CertiKOS left to untrusted userspace.

\subsection{The Practical Horizon}

A verified kernel that runs no existing software is a laboratory
artifact.  Telix's personality subsystem (Chapter~\ref{ch:personality})
is designed to run unmodified Linux and Windows binaries through
userspace ABI emulation servers, giving the kernel a practical path
to real workloads.  The economic motivation is straightforward:
the operating systems that dominate deployed computing (Linux,
Windows, and increasingly OpenHarmony and Zircon) were designed
before formal verification, before Rust, and before the current
understanding of TLB-pressure economics.  A kernel that matches
their ABI compatibility while providing stronger isolation
guarantees, sublinear metadata scaling, and verifiable correctness
for its security-critical paths is an existence proof that the
next generation can do better — and the existence proof is what
Telix aims to be.

\section{Core Architectural Principles}

Telix synthesizes its architectural foundation around four primary
domain goals:

\begin{description}
    \item[Formal Verification:] Formally verify via \CoqRocq\ or other
	    theorem provers.

    \item[Hardware Partitioning and Safety Domain Isolation:] Systems
	    are partitioned per the Barrelfish multikernel model
	    \footfullcite{baum09} into decoupled message-passing nodes
	    (one kernel instance per NUMA node, rather than per core).
	    Within each multikernel partition, Telix selectively adapts
	    the \Asterinas\ \framekernel\ technique
	    \footfullcite{peng24}: thin, performance-critical server
	    layers are pulled into the main kernel address space and
	    protected by Rust's compile-time safety guarantees rather
	    than by hardware MMU isolation.  The remaining servers
	    retain their own address spaces, kept out of the
	    framekernel TCB.  This hybrid boundary is the most
	    experimental aspect of the design (see
	    \S\ref{ch:control-plane}); if the language-level boundary
	    proves unsound, the two latency-motivated components
	    (the LLFree allocator and scheduler management loop) are
	    promoted to userspace servers behind MMU boundaries,
	    while the privileged set — page-table manipulation,
	    shared-PT management, TLB shootdown, and IOMMU management
	    — remains in the kernel, exactly where it sits in seL4,
	    L4, or any microkernel.

    \item[Hybrid Continuation-Passing and Network-Structured Control
	    Plane:] For fully asynchronous operation with efficiency
	    within shared memory domains while also cluster-ready by
	    virtue of discipline maintained for IPC.

    \item[Metadata-Light Virtual Memory Management:] Virtual memory
	    overhead is constrained to sublinear scaling relative to
	    physical memory size. Telix combines superpaging
	    \footfullcite{nava02}, shared page tables
	    \footfullcite{ko03}, software-managed inverted page tables
	    \footfullcite{tall95, huck93}, and BSD/Linux
	    \pageclustering\ algorithms \footfullcite{baba79, dick01}.
	    Additionally, traditional page descriptor arrays are
	    eliminated by adapting main-memory database morsel-driven
	    allocation\footfullcite{kemp14}.

    \item[Hybrid Workload Co-Scheduling:] Thread execution uses $M:N$
	    threading via Scheduler Activations \footfullcite{ande92}
	    coupled with Earliest Eligible Virtual Deadline First
	    (EEVDF) proportional allocation \footfullcite{stoi95} and
	    parallel gang co-scheduling \footfullcite{oust82, feit92}.

    \item[Asynchronous Zero-Copy I/O and Compatibility:] Inter-process
	    communication and system invocations rely on lock-free fast
	    buffers (Fbufs) \footfullcite{drus93} and completion-driven
	    queues \footfullcite{krie06, ande91}. Multiple binary
	    compatibility interfaces (Linux, Windows) are supported
	    through a dynamic personality redirection subsystem
	    \footfullcite{gerb01, golu91, zajc93, rash87, milo93}.
\end{description}

\section{Target Hardware Architectures \& Execution Models}
\label{sec:target_architectures}

Telix is designed to target modern 64-bit multi-core hardware platforms.
Rather than tailoring kernel abstractions to a single ISA, Telix defines a
uniform execution model while accommodating architectural differences in MMU
topologies, memory consistency models, and hardware capability enforcement.

\begin{description}
    \item[RISC-V (\texttt{rv64g} / \texttt{rv64gc}):] Serving as the
	    primary open reference architecture. RISC-V provides clean
	    software-managed page table options (Sv39/Sv48),
	    standardized formal Sail ISA definitions
	    \footcite{armstrong19}, and an extensible ISA interface
	    well-suited for framekernel isolation domains. The Svnapot
	    extensions are of particular interest for the sake of
	    demonstrating Telix' abilities.

    \item[ARMv8-A / ARMv9-A (AArch64):] The primary production target
	    for high-core-count datacenter nodes and modern IoT devices.
	    Telix explicitly accounts for AArch64's weakly-ordered
	    memory model when synthesizing cross-core synchronization
	    ring buffers and page table updates \footcite{sosp21vrm}.

    \item[LoongArch:] Mandatory because China is the future.
    \item[MIPS:] The basic picture of sanity.

    \item[Capability Hardware (CHERI / Arm Morello):] Target platform
	    for high-assurance deployments. Hardware fine-grained
	    capabilities allow Telix to enforce spatial and temporal
	    memory safety directly in hardware, complementing Rust's
	    software safety guarantees within framekernels
	    \footcite{katamaran23}. The MIPS \texttt{PageGrain}
	    extension is of particular interest for the sake of
	    demonstrating Telix' abilities.

    \item[x86-64:] Supported primarily for broad compatibility on
	    commodity hardware. While x86-64 provides Total Store Order
	    (TSO) memory semantics simplifying ring buffer mechanics,
	    its rigid multi-level page table hierarchy demands shared
	    page table tree strategies \footcite{ko03}. Its segregated
	    TLBs also defeat much of the purpose of superpaging.
\end{description}

Some potential further ports might be to:
\begin{description}
	\item[Alpha:] DEC was the best.
	\item[IA64:] The best architecture ever until corrupted with
		the HPW and similar horrors.
	\item[Motorola 68000:]
	\item[OpenRISC:]
	\item[PA-RISC:]
	\item[POWER:]
	\item[SPARC:] Beauty incarnated as a microprocessor.
	\item[VAX:] The full 512~B page size \pageclustering\ experience.
\end{description}

\section{Hardware Acceleration and Architectural Extensions}
\label{sec:hardware_extensions}

Telix explicitly relies on specialized ISA features to eliminate
traditional OS microarchitectural bottlenecks:

\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{RISC-V \texttt{Svnapot} Extension}] \footcite{riscv_priv_1_12}:
    Standard 4~KiB page structures often induce heavy TLB miss rates, while 2~MiB superpages lead to severe internal memory fragmentation. Telix leverages the RISC-V \texttt{Svnapot} (Naturally Aligned Power-of-Two) extension—specifically using the $N$-bit in Page Table Entries (PTEs) to represent 64~KiB contiguous physical allocations within a single TLB entry footprint.

    \item[\textbf{MIPS PageGrain Extension}] \footcite{mips_pra_6_02}:
    For low-footprint embedded and Personal Area Network (PAN) nodes, standard 4~KiB pages waste substantial memory. Telix utilizes the MIPS \texttt{PageGrain} register (CP0 Register 5, Select 1) to enable sub-4~KiB (1~KiB) page boundaries, alongside hardware-enforced Read-Inhibit (\texttt{RI}) and Execute-Inhibit (\texttt{XI}) permissions.

    \item[\textbf{CHERI Architectural Capabilities}] \footcite{watson19cheri}:
    Capability Hardware Enhanced RISC Instructions (CHERI) replace conventional virtual address pointers with bounded, unforgeable hardware capabilities. In Telix's Framekernel design, CHERI enables sub-process isolation within a single kernel address space, guaranteeing spatial and temporal memory safety at hardware speed without software bounds-checking overhead.

    \item[\textbf{Custom Inverted-Page-Table MMU (satp modes 14/15)}]:
    A forward-looking RISC-V extension replacing the radix-tree walker
    with a partner-hashed inverted page table, a POWER9-style segment
    lookaside buffer, and residue-partitioned TLBs.  It spans 42
    distinct translation sizes (41 superpage sizes above a 256~B base
    page — half the VAX's 512~B) while preserving large-page TLB
    reach through \pageclustering.  The design is modelled in Sail and proved in
    Rocq as part of the Tessera hardware model, with a QEMU
    simulation branch.  It is detailed in \S\ref{sec:custom_mmu} and
    is a research prototype requiring RISC-V ratification of the new
    \texttt{satp} encodings.
\end{description}
